/// Class X mathematics, from a chapter-wise school question bank.
///
/// Chosen because this is his own ground: he taught maths to classes 10 to 12
/// for a working lifetime, and these are the chapters he taught -- real
/// numbers, polynomials, quadratic equations, arithmetic progressions,
/// coordinate geometry, trigonometry, mensuration, statistics, probability.
///
/// Every numerical answer here is computed, not copied. That is not caution
/// for its own sake: the source worksheets have real errors in their option
/// sets. Asked for the chance that a card drawn from 1 to 50 is divisible by
/// 7, the book offers 8/25, 9/25, 6/25 and 3/25 -- and the answer is 7/50,
/// which is not among them. Those look like options left over from the 1-to-25
/// version of the same question. So the book was used to decide which topics
/// belong in Class X, and nothing else: the arithmetic is generated, and each
/// answer was then re-derived a second time by a separate route to catch a
/// mistake in the generator itself.
///
/// The questions that are not computations -- identities, definitions, the
/// conditions on a discriminant, how many tangents can be drawn -- are
/// written by hand, and the trigonometric claims among them were checked
/// numerically before being written down.
///
/// These are mostly difficulty 3, which is deliberate. The hard pool is the
/// one that decides how long he can play before a question comes round again,
/// and it was the smallest of the three.
